[{:db/id 17592186147415 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-83497943-4dd9-5808-bc36-db659744aaad"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-83497943-4dd9-5808-bc36-db659744aaad" :question/id #uuid "83497943-4dd9-5808-bc36-db659744aaad" :question/math-academy-id "q-166797" :question/problem "Expressed as a single fraction, the exact area of the plot of land is$\\,${{field-1}}\n${\\text{mi}}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "The exact area of land is ${(\\frac{4}{5})}^{2}\\,{\\text{mi}}^{2}.$\n\nWriting ${(\\frac{4}{5})}^{2}$ with multiplication signs, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{4}{5})}^{2} & ={{\\frac{4}{5}\\times \\frac{4}{5}}_{⏟}}_{2\\text{ times}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\times 4}{5\\times 5} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{4}{5})}^{2}=\\frac{16}{25}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-40a52d3e-493d-5f72-b1e1-ef146d9e54e7"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-40a52d3e-493d-5f72-b1e1-ef146d9e54e7" :answer-field/id #uuid "40a52d3e-493d-5f72-b1e1-ef146d9e54e7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-47cde53f-47d7-5ae4-8572-e9ac9bf6754d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-47cde53f-47d7-5ae4-8572-e9ac9bf6754d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-47cde53f-47d7-5ae4-8572-e9ac9bf6754d" :answer/id #uuid "47cde53f-47d7-5ae4-8572-e9ac9bf6754d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{16}{25}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186054858 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-215d1b37-57d3-50ad-9730-b954c879d087"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-215d1b37-57d3-50ad-9730-b954c879d087" :question/id #uuid "215d1b37-57d3-50ad-9730-b954c879d087" :question/math-academy-id "q-166798" :question/problem "Building company policy states that each ton of cement mixture should contain at least $0.7$ tons of sand. What's the minimum amount of sand in $1.2$ tons of cement mixture? Express your answer as a decimal.\n\nThere will be a minimum of\n{{field-1}}\ntons of sand in $1.2$ tons of cement mixture." :question/worked-solution "To determine how much sand is in $1.2$ tons of the cement mixture, we have to multiply $1.2$ by $0.7.$\n\nWe proceed by multiplying the two numbers just as we would with whole numbers.\n\nNotice that the second number has a leading $0$, which we ignore during the multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{aligned} &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\, &  \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,.2\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\times \\,\\,\\,\\, &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,.7\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,8\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now count the total number of decimal places in the two factors.\n\nThere is $1$ decimal place in $1.2$ and there is $1$ decimal place in $0.7.$\nTherefore, their product will have $1+1=2$ decimal places.\n\nSo, we take our value of $84$ and add a decimal point to make a number with $2$ decimal places.\n\n$$\n\\,\\overset{↓}{.}\\,\\,\\,{{84}_{⏟}}_{2\\text{ digits}}\n$$\n\nHence, $1.2\\times 0.7=0.84.$\n\nWe conclude that there are $0.84$ tons of sand in $1.2$ tons of the cement mixture." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a95f5153-0f06-5465-8815-86cb9c85319c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a95f5153-0f06-5465-8815-86cb9c85319c" :answer-field/id #uuid "a95f5153-0f06-5465-8815-86cb9c85319c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-8d1c2dc7-edf8-570a-812f-d15c687b21fd" :answer-field/choices ["answer-8d1c2dc7-edf8-570a-812f-d15c687b21fd"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8d1c2dc7-edf8-570a-812f-d15c687b21fd" :answer/id #uuid "8d1c2dc7-edf8-570a-812f-d15c687b21fd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.84" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186138368 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-6535052d-d80b-57f3-9a80-25b64c4c2047"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-6535052d-d80b-57f3-9a80-25b64c4c2047" :question/id #uuid "6535052d-d80b-57f3-9a80-25b64c4c2047" :question/math-academy-id "q-166799" :question/problem "The building company combines $1.6$ tons of sand with $0.69$ tons of cement to make their cement mixture. What is the total weight of the mixture? Express your answer as a decimal.\n\nThe total weight of the mixture is\n{{field-1}}\ntons." :question/worked-solution "To find the total weight of the mixture, we need to calculate the sum $1.6+0.69.$\n\nThere is $1$ decimal place in $1.6$ and $2$ decimal places in $0.69.$\n\nTo add the numbers, they both need to have the same number of decimals. Hence, we rewrite the first number so that it has $2$ decimal places:\n\n$$\n1.6=1.60\n$$\n\nNext, we line up the decimal points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,.\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,6\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,+\\,\\,\\,\\, &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,.\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,6\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,9\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,.\\,\\,\\,\\, &  & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by adding the two numbers just as we would with whole numbers:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\, &  &  \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,.\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,6\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,+\\,\\,\\,\\, &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,.\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,6\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,9\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,.\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,9\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total weight of the mixture is $2.29$ tons." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-02e8ab80-f28a-5a91-a4ee-5bbed7cc0309"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-02e8ab80-f28a-5a91-a4ee-5bbed7cc0309" :answer-field/id #uuid "02e8ab80-f28a-5a91-a4ee-5bbed7cc0309" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-b46221bb-c9ba-5b3b-af2a-aefe7602f145" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b46221bb-c9ba-5b3b-af2a-aefe7602f145"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b46221bb-c9ba-5b3b-af2a-aefe7602f145" :answer/id #uuid "b46221bb-c9ba-5b3b-af2a-aefe7602f145" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2.29" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186054862 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-ee99225f-5b1d-5184-84fe-979d439fc52f"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-ee99225f-5b1d-5184-84fe-979d439fc52f" :question/id #uuid "ee99225f-5b1d-5184-84fe-979d439fc52f" :question/math-academy-id "q-166800" :question/problem "Of the cement mixture prepared, $1.87$ tons were used to construct the houses. How much cement mixture is left over?\n\nThere are\n{{field-1}}\ntons of cement mixture are left over." :question/worked-solution "In part 3, we found that the company prepared $2.29$ tons of cement mixture.\n\nTo determine the amount of cement mixture remaining, we need to calculate $2.29-1.87.$\n\nFirst, we line up the decimal points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,.\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,9\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,-\\,\\,\\,\\, &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,.\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,8\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,7\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,.\\,\\,\\,\\, &  & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we proceed by subtracting the two numbers just as we would with whole numbers:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\, &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned} \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\, &  \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,.\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,9\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,-\\,\\,\\,\\, &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,.\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,8\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,7\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,.\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $0.42$ tons of cement mixture are left over." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f34c47c7-21ad-5287-bc9c-731eacee1cb5"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f34c47c7-21ad-5287-bc9c-731eacee1cb5" :answer-field/id #uuid "f34c47c7-21ad-5287-bc9c-731eacee1cb5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-bcf24fbb-54b2-594a-834d-965cf812e613" :answer-field/choices ["answer-bcf24fbb-54b2-594a-834d-965cf812e613"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bcf24fbb-54b2-594a-834d-965cf812e613" :answer/id #uuid "bcf24fbb-54b2-594a-834d-965cf812e613" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.42" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186073705 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-ecc24b66-1c17-5f12-9e7e-a64bd2e2d9c8"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-ecc24b66-1c17-5f12-9e7e-a64bd2e2d9c8" :question/id #uuid "ecc24b66-1c17-5f12-9e7e-a64bd2e2d9c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-166801" :question/problem "One of the workers cuts $3\\,\\frac{3}{4}\\,\\text{ft}$ from a pipe measuring $5\\,\\frac{1}{8}\\,\\text{ft}.$ What is the length of the remaining pipe? Express your answer as a mixed number.\n\nThe length of the remaining pipe is$\\,$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{ft}.$" :question/worked-solution "The length of the remaining pipe is $(5\\,\\frac{1}{8}-3\\,\\frac{3}{4})\\text{ft}.$\n\nWriting $5\\,\\frac{1}{8}$ as an improper fraction, we have\n\n$$\n5\\,\\frac{1}{8}=\\frac{5\\times 8+1}{8}=\\frac{41}{8}.\n$$\n\nWriting $3\\,\\frac{3}{4}$ as an improper fraction, we have\n\n$$\n3\\,\\frac{3}{4}=\\frac{3\\times 4+3}{4}=\\frac{15}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our problem now is to work out the following:\n\n$$\n\\frac{41}{8}-\\frac{15}{4}\n$$\n\nTo subtract two fractions with unlike denominators, we need to express each fraction as an equivalent fraction with a common denominator.\n\nIn this question, we can make a common denominator of $8.$\n\nTo put $\\frac{15}{4}$ over a denominator of $8,$ we multiply the numerator and denominator by $2:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{15}{4}=\\frac{15\\times 2}{4\\times 2}=\\frac{30}{8}\n$$\n\nWe can now subtract the fractions. We keep the denominator the same, and we subtract the numerators:\n\n$$\n\\frac{41}{8}-\\frac{30}{8}=\\frac{11}{8}\n$$\n\nFinally, we convert back to a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\frac{11}{8}=1\\,\\text{R}\\,3=1\\,\\frac{3}{8}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $1\\,\\frac{3}{8}\\,\\text{ft}$ of pipe remains." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-89b6a21c-a813-55f9-b362-1ae1f8cff07b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-89b6a21c-a813-55f9-b362-1ae1f8cff07b" :answer-field/id #uuid "89b6a21c-a813-55f9-b362-1ae1f8cff07b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-dda3fa24-a3e1-52ae-b9a9-bebd3842e781" :answer-field/choices ["answer-dda3fa24-a3e1-52ae-b9a9-bebd3842e781"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dda3fa24-a3e1-52ae-b9a9-bebd3842e781" :answer/id #uuid "dda3fa24-a3e1-52ae-b9a9-bebd3842e781" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1\\,\\frac{3}{8}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186073676 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-1e21dfbe-7205-50ca-b839-4d1dcb287bbd"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-1e21dfbe-7205-50ca-b839-4d1dcb287bbd" :question/id #uuid "1e21dfbe-7205-50ca-b839-4d1dcb287bbd" :question/math-academy-id "q-166802" :question/problem "The company used $104.7\\,\\text{ft}$ of piping to construct $5$ identical houses. Estimate the length of piping used for each home by rounding the total piping length to the nearest whole number.\n\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{ft}.$" :question/worked-solution "To estimate the length of piping used for each of the $5$ houses, we need to find an approximation for $104.7÷5.$\n\nSo, we round the dividend to the nearest whole number:\n\n$$\n104.7\\to 105\n$$\n\nNow, we divide $105$ by $5$ as usual:\n\n$$\n105÷5=21\n$$\n\nTherefore, the construction of each house used approximately $21\\,\\text{ft}$ of pipes." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-07b47d55-bb99-56d8-b25e-ea951aea2d16"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-07b47d55-bb99-56d8-b25e-ea951aea2d16" :answer-field/id #uuid "07b47d55-bb99-56d8-b25e-ea951aea2d16" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-988f552d-7fef-5802-8752-162edc8a6d1b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-988f552d-7fef-5802-8752-162edc8a6d1b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-988f552d-7fef-5802-8752-162edc8a6d1b" :answer/id #uuid "988f552d-7fef-5802-8752-162edc8a6d1b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "21" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "multistep-10b798da-88e8-599e-9a89-b5ff9c361f9a" :multistep/id #uuid "10b798da-88e8-599e-9a89-b5ff9c361f9a" :multistep/context "A construction company is building $5$ identical houses on a ${(\\frac{4}{5})}^{2}$ square mile plot of land." :multistep/steps ["step-19f866bd-8dc2-5477-992d-1473853d0c16" "step-378f9eb4-6689-57a1-ace0-5895d0bf21e7" "step-ae5ab332-2aa1-5052-911b-e0eb68db34fe" "step-9a62d115-e7f9-5bb0-b1b3-7e96b43d7656" "step-1f2d7e1e-53f6-5c92-89c3-7c3117f65f5c" "step-3e352b3e-ae0c-57b9-9219-0f7ceced29eb"] :multistep/first-step "step-19f866bd-8dc2-5477-992d-1473853d0c16" :db/ensure [:multistep/validate]} {:db/id "step-19f866bd-8dc2-5477-992d-1473853d0c16" :step/id #uuid "19f866bd-8dc2-5477-992d-1473853d0c16" :step/content "question-83497943-4dd9-5808-bc36-db659744aaad" :step/next "step-378f9eb4-6689-57a1-ace0-5895d0bf21e7" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-378f9eb4-6689-57a1-ace0-5895d0bf21e7" :step/id #uuid "378f9eb4-6689-57a1-ace0-5895d0bf21e7" :step/content "question-215d1b37-57d3-50ad-9730-b954c879d087" :step/next "step-ae5ab332-2aa1-5052-911b-e0eb68db34fe" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-ae5ab332-2aa1-5052-911b-e0eb68db34fe" :step/id #uuid "ae5ab332-2aa1-5052-911b-e0eb68db34fe" :step/content "question-6535052d-d80b-57f3-9a80-25b64c4c2047" :step/next "step-9a62d115-e7f9-5bb0-b1b3-7e96b43d7656" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-9a62d115-e7f9-5bb0-b1b3-7e96b43d7656" :step/id #uuid "9a62d115-e7f9-5bb0-b1b3-7e96b43d7656" :step/content "question-ee99225f-5b1d-5184-84fe-979d439fc52f" :step/next "step-1f2d7e1e-53f6-5c92-89c3-7c3117f65f5c" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-1f2d7e1e-53f6-5c92-89c3-7c3117f65f5c" :step/id #uuid "1f2d7e1e-53f6-5c92-89c3-7c3117f65f5c" :step/content "question-ecc24b66-1c17-5f12-9e7e-a64bd2e2d9c8" :step/next "step-3e352b3e-ae0c-57b9-9219-0f7ceced29eb" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-3e352b3e-ae0c-57b9-9219-0f7ceced29eb" :step/id #uuid "3e352b3e-ae0c-57b9-9219-0f7ceced29eb" :step/content "question-1e21dfbe-7205-50ca-b839-4d1dcb287bbd" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "assigned-problem-187f6efc-19d9-5f09-84b1-1bc3362246d3" :assigned-problem/id #uuid "187f6efc-19d9-5f09-84b1-1bc3362246d3" :assigned-problem/content "multistep-10b798da-88e8-599e-9a89-b5ff9c361f9a" :assigned-problem/topic-coverage [17592186048381 17592186049194 17592186049484 17592186049097 17592186050701 17592186050177] :db/ensure [:assigned-problem/validate]} {:db/id "activity-8832ccc5-5062-53eb-9aa3-cff46feb853b" :activity/id #uuid "8832ccc5-5062-53eb-9aa3-cff46feb853b" :activity/title "Decimal and Fraction Operations Applied to Commercial Settings" :activity/type :activity.type/assignment :activity/steps ["step-18e873e5-9337-506c-b540-e1fc8d47545f"] :activity/first-step "step-18e873e5-9337-506c-b540-e1fc8d47545f" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-18e873e5-9337-506c-b540-e1fc8d47545f" :step/id #uuid "18e873e5-9337-506c-b540-e1fc8d47545f" :step/content "assigned-problem-187f6efc-19d9-5f09-84b1-1bc3362246d3" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186062749 :answer-field/choices 17592186062752] [:db/retract 17592186062749 :answer-field/choices 17592186062753] [:db/retract 17592186062749 :answer-field/choices 17592186062754] [:db/retract 17592186062749 :answer-field/choices 17592186062750] [:db/retract 17592186062749 :answer-field/choices 17592186062751] {:db/id 17592186064193 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-16ef2d7f-f052-5e96-9861-83b3cce4c0bd" "question-7ab827a9-09e7-59f8-93b4-f0c91318eeaa" "question-8670623f-a7f4-578e-914d-6583efa2a94c"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186063969 :question/problem "Solve for $x$ where $2x - y = 5y - z.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to isolate $x,$ so we will treat the other variables $y$ and $z$ as constants. We can use the addition and multiplication principles to solve for $x.$\n\nFirst, we add $y$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t2x - y &= 5y - z \\[5pt]\n\t\t2x - y + y &= 5y - z + y \\[5pt]\n\t\t2x &= 6y - z\n\t}\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, we divide both sides by $2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t2x &= 6y - z \\[5pt]\n\t\t\\dfrac {2x} 2 &= \\dfrac{6y - z} {2} \\[5pt]\n\t\tx &= \\dfrac{6y - z} {2}\n\t}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064194 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-14cd0a28-442e-5455-9aaf-cc966e4aa8e5" "question-237b013e-fcd6-5777-9bce-b39ac50c9f8e"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186063970 :question/worked-solution "We need to isolate $m,$ so we will treat the other variables $E$ and $C$ as constants. Since $m$ is being multiplied by $C,$ we need to divide both sides of the equation by $C$:\n![](images/27/27683350a3eb25bd3962e12724a01f09350d4c62ba85afb137df8cff27992be7.svg)\nThus, $m = \\dfrac E C.$\n\n**Note:** This result assumes that $C\\neq 0.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064195 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-de5d5814-9f69-514d-a764-4ee1edcf544a" "question-220b7284-89df-58d8-b7fb-14c6a632a4f4"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186063971 :question/problem "The total energy $E$ of a body moving with a (squared) velocity $V$ can be approximated using the equation $E = mC + \\dfrac{1}{2}mV.$ Find an expression for $V$ in terms of the other quantities." :question/worked-solution "We need to isolate $V,$ so we will treat the other variables $E,$ $C,$ and $m$ as constants. We can use the addition and multiplication principles to solve for $V.$\n\nFirst, we subtract $mC$ from both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E & =mC+\\frac{1}{2}mV \\\\ E-mC & =mC+\\frac{1}{2}mV-mC \\\\ E-mC & =\\frac{1}{2}mV\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we multiply by $2$ to get rid of the fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E-mC & =\\frac{1}{2}mV \\\\ 2\\cdot (E-mC) & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}mV \\\\ 2(E-mC) & =mV\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide by $m$ to get $V$ alone:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(E-mC) & =mV \\\\ \\frac{2(E-mC)}{m} & =\\frac{mV}{m} \\\\ \\frac{2(E-mC)}{m} & =V\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $V = \\dfrac{2(E-mC)}{m}.$\n\n**Note:** This result assumes that $m\\neq 0.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-16ef2d7f-f052-5e96-9861-83b3cce4c0bd" :question/id #uuid "16ef2d7f-f052-5e96-9861-83b3cce4c0bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-6272" :question/problem "Solve for $a$ where $c=3a+7b.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to isolate $a,$ so we will treat the other variables $b$ and $c$ constants. We can use the addition and multiplication principles to solve for $a.$\n\nFirst, we subtract $7b$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =3a+7b \\\\ c-7b & =3a+7b-7b \\\\ c-7b & =3a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide both sides by $3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3a & =c-7b \\\\ \\frac{3a}{3} & =\\frac{c-7b}{3} \\\\ a & =\\frac{c-7b}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d99aa76c-d573-53bf-9199-ad883ba2b9fc"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d99aa76c-d573-53bf-9199-ad883ba2b9fc" :answer-field/id #uuid "d99aa76c-d573-53bf-9199-ad883ba2b9fc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-767ff254-9406-539d-9543-9df59a37d2cd" :answer-field/choices ["answer-fad7c0e4-a858-510b-8bfb-0a65e5cb5862" "answer-87e00313-7fa3-5ad4-b7f0-80d16185aea5" "answer-256c4e0a-88bd-5462-b34b-8e0201196256" "answer-767ff254-9406-539d-9543-9df59a37d2cd" "answer-8a19160c-90ed-5621-b707-63db2d30d54d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fad7c0e4-a858-510b-8bfb-0a65e5cb5862" :answer/id #uuid "fad7c0e4-a858-510b-8bfb-0a65e5cb5862" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "a=3c-21b" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-87e00313-7fa3-5ad4-b7f0-80d16185aea5" :answer/id #uuid "87e00313-7fa3-5ad4-b7f0-80d16185aea5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "a=3c-7b" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-256c4e0a-88bd-5462-b34b-8e0201196256" :answer/id #uuid "256c4e0a-88bd-5462-b34b-8e0201196256" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "a=\\frac{3}{c-7b}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-767ff254-9406-539d-9543-9df59a37d2cd" :answer/id #uuid "767ff254-9406-539d-9543-9df59a37d2cd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "a=\\frac{c-7b}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8a19160c-90ed-5621-b707-63db2d30d54d" :answer/id #uuid "8a19160c-90ed-5621-b707-63db2d30d54d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "a=c-7b" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064782 :question/problem "Make $c$ the subject of the equation $-6a=9b-2c.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to isolate $c,$ so we will treat the other variables $a$ and $b$ as constants. We can use the addition and multiplication principles to solve for $c.$\n\nFirst, we add $2c$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6a & =9b-2c \\\\ -6a+2c & =9b-2c+2c \\\\ -6a+2c & =9b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we add $6a$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6a+2c & =9b \\\\ -6a+2c+6a & =9b+6a \\\\ 2c & =9b+6a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide both sides by $2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2c & =9b+6a \\\\ \\frac{2c}{2} & =\\frac{9b+6a}{2} \\\\ c & =\\frac{9b+6a}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186062749 :answer-field/correct "answer-ce723e6b-a7f1-5c83-93c0-26b1992c7d0b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c3c54d08-3027-5477-8e6e-eddd2f2d7c2d" "answer-b0f48295-a359-57d7-a83b-45f2b69c8918" "answer-8a235d76-c366-542d-9489-73cd1cbe8354" "answer-ce723e6b-a7f1-5c83-93c0-26b1992c7d0b" "answer-90beab16-0f3f-5729-9a4f-2ff5756dab6a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c3c54d08-3027-5477-8e6e-eddd2f2d7c2d" :answer/id #uuid "c3c54d08-3027-5477-8e6e-eddd2f2d7c2d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "c=9b+3a" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b0f48295-a359-57d7-a83b-45f2b69c8918" :answer/id #uuid "b0f48295-a359-57d7-a83b-45f2b69c8918" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "c=9b+6a+2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8a235d76-c366-542d-9489-73cd1cbe8354" :answer/id #uuid "8a235d76-c366-542d-9489-73cd1cbe8354" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "c=9b+6a-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ce723e6b-a7f1-5c83-93c0-26b1992c7d0b" :answer/id #uuid "ce723e6b-a7f1-5c83-93c0-26b1992c7d0b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "c=\\frac{9b+6a}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-90beab16-0f3f-5729-9a4f-2ff5756dab6a" :answer/id #uuid "90beab16-0f3f-5729-9a4f-2ff5756dab6a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "c=\\frac{9b-6a}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-7ab827a9-09e7-59f8-93b4-f0c91318eeaa" :question/id #uuid "7ab827a9-09e7-59f8-93b4-f0c91318eeaa" :question/math-academy-id "q-215068" :question/problem "If $y=5w+7x,$ then $x=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We need to isolate $x,$ so we will treat the other variables $w$ and $y$ as constants. We can use the addition and multiplication principles to solve for $x.$\n\nFirst, we subtract $5w$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =5w+7x \\\\ y-5w & =5w+7x-5w \\\\ y-5w & =7x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide both sides by $7:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7x & =y-5w \\\\ \\frac{7x}{7} & =\\frac{y-5w}{7} \\\\ x & =\\frac{y-5w}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-dd35897b-af9b-538f-9da8-73a25cda3699"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-dd35897b-af9b-538f-9da8-73a25cda3699" :answer-field/id #uuid "dd35897b-af9b-538f-9da8-73a25cda3699" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-088681b8-dece-5943-86ef-0ce140124469" :answer-field/choices ["answer-088681b8-dece-5943-86ef-0ce140124469"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-088681b8-dece-5943-86ef-0ce140124469" :answer/id #uuid "088681b8-dece-5943-86ef-0ce140124469" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{y-5w}{7}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-8670623f-a7f4-578e-914d-6583efa2a94c" :question/id #uuid "8670623f-a7f4-578e-914d-6583efa2a94c" :question/math-academy-id "q-104505" :question/problem "Make $k$ the subject of the equation $-2h=2m-3k.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to isolate $k,$ so we will treat the other variables $h$ and $m$ as constants. We can use the addition and multiplication principles to solve for $k.$\n\nFirst, we add $3k$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2h & =2m-3k \\\\ -2h+3k & =2m-3k+3k \\\\ 3k-2h & =2m\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we add $2h$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3k-2h & =2m \\\\ 3k-2h+2h & =2m+2h \\\\ 3k & =2m+2h\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide both sides by $3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3k & =2m+2h \\\\ \\frac{3k}{3} & =\\frac{2m+2h}{3} \\\\ k & =\\frac{2(m+h)}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b4d356cb-97ed-53b6-8ae4-08e38186803d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b4d356cb-97ed-53b6-8ae4-08e38186803d" :answer-field/id #uuid "b4d356cb-97ed-53b6-8ae4-08e38186803d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-e809a959-a7e3-5017-bec0-23321eab7046" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e47626ff-f26d-54cd-a4ab-f41b3c4645bd" "answer-753ef4e9-c18a-5f70-917d-3c3920d8ca21" "answer-e809a959-a7e3-5017-bec0-23321eab7046" "answer-7deab777-c2dd-5601-b8e7-37ac5578cab3" "answer-16950e72-b362-5e6f-88f9-2a640ed97951"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e47626ff-f26d-54cd-a4ab-f41b3c4645bd" :answer/id #uuid "e47626ff-f26d-54cd-a4ab-f41b3c4645bd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "k=-\\frac{m+h}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-753ef4e9-c18a-5f70-917d-3c3920d8ca21" :answer/id #uuid "753ef4e9-c18a-5f70-917d-3c3920d8ca21" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "k=\\frac{2(h-m)}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e809a959-a7e3-5017-bec0-23321eab7046" :answer/id #uuid "e809a959-a7e3-5017-bec0-23321eab7046" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "k=\\frac{2(m+h)}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7deab777-c2dd-5601-b8e7-37ac5578cab3" :answer/id #uuid "7deab777-c2dd-5601-b8e7-37ac5578cab3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "k=\\frac{2}{3m-2h}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-16950e72-b362-5e6f-88f9-2a640ed97951" :answer/id #uuid "16950e72-b362-5e6f-88f9-2a640ed97951" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "k=\\frac{m-2h}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-14cd0a28-442e-5455-9aaf-cc966e4aa8e5" :question/id #uuid "14cd0a28-442e-5455-9aaf-cc966e4aa8e5" :question/math-academy-id "q-67262" :question/problem "The formula to calculate the distance that an object in uniform rectilinear motion has traveled is given by\n\n$$\nd=v\\cdot t,\n$$\n\nwhere $v$ represents the velocity of the moving object and $t$ represents time.\n\nWhat is the formula for velocity in terms of distance and time?" :question/worked-solution "We need to isolate $v,$ so we will treat the other variables $d$ and $t$ as constants. Since $v$ is being multiplied by $t,$ we need to divide both sides of the equation by $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =v\\cdot t \\\\ \\frac{d}{t} & =\\frac{v\\cdot t}{t} \\\\ \\frac{d}{t} & =\\frac{v\\cdot \\cancel{t}}{\\cancel{t}} \\\\ \\frac{d}{t} & =v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $v=\\frac{d}{t}.$\n\n**Note:** This result assumes that $t\\ne 0.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-72ae3726-b8b0-5976-82de-d20849cd73be"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-72ae3726-b8b0-5976-82de-d20849cd73be" :answer-field/id #uuid "72ae3726-b8b0-5976-82de-d20849cd73be" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-66ea85cf-8a45-52b0-afc6-5302893f8c64" :answer-field/choices ["answer-66ea85cf-8a45-52b0-afc6-5302893f8c64" "answer-71975e50-0db1-56f1-b56c-2415d3dc4ef8" "answer-d69843dc-5143-515e-8912-eb43f77877dd" "answer-fb3022ea-5ac3-50d9-99da-0805732230e4" "answer-d1565845-fb01-5dfd-baa4-d20077d7f91a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-66ea85cf-8a45-52b0-afc6-5302893f8c64" :answer/id #uuid "66ea85cf-8a45-52b0-afc6-5302893f8c64" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "v=\\frac{d}{t}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-71975e50-0db1-56f1-b56c-2415d3dc4ef8" :answer/id #uuid "71975e50-0db1-56f1-b56c-2415d3dc4ef8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "v=\\frac{t}{v}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d69843dc-5143-515e-8912-eb43f77877dd" :answer/id #uuid "d69843dc-5143-515e-8912-eb43f77877dd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "v=d+t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fb3022ea-5ac3-50d9-99da-0805732230e4" :answer/id #uuid "fb3022ea-5ac3-50d9-99da-0805732230e4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "v=d-t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d1565845-fb01-5dfd-baa4-d20077d7f91a" :answer/id #uuid "d1565845-fb01-5dfd-baa4-d20077d7f91a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "v=d\\cdot t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-237b013e-fcd6-5777-9bce-b39ac50c9f8e" :question/id #uuid "237b013e-fcd6-5777-9bce-b39ac50c9f8e" :question/math-academy-id "q-75547" :question/problem "The surface area of a cylinder is given by \n\n$$\nA=2\\pi rh\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is the radius and $h$ is the height of the cylinder.\n\nWhat is the formula for the height of the cylinder in terms of the other variables?" :question/worked-solution "We need to isolate $h,$ so we will treat the other variables $A$ and $r$ as constants.\n\nFirst, we rearrange and then divide both sides by $2\\pi r$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =2\\pi rh \\\\ A & =(2\\pi r)h \\\\ \\frac{A}{2\\pi r} & =\\frac{(2\\pi r)h}{2\\pi r} \\\\ \\frac{A}{2\\pi r} & =h\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $h=\\frac{A}{2\\pi r}.$\n\n**Note:** This result assumes that $r\\ne 0.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-6d4cbd72-7cba-578e-b4d1-9a4441fcaa2e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-6d4cbd72-7cba-578e-b4d1-9a4441fcaa2e" :answer-field/id #uuid "6d4cbd72-7cba-578e-b4d1-9a4441fcaa2e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-b984c89f-22b6-5f39-a9e4-709f228a20bf" :answer-field/choices ["answer-44a39803-ff77-57fe-b5c2-efc3892518ae" "answer-dd8c229b-04e9-5db8-b9b0-7619b9b60f94" "answer-b984c89f-22b6-5f39-a9e4-709f228a20bf" "answer-509ef183-713a-5b80-9cea-d36045a8b4db" "answer-5f309670-2000-5f40-9e63-064e5228ef92"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-44a39803-ff77-57fe-b5c2-efc3892518ae" :answer/id #uuid "44a39803-ff77-57fe-b5c2-efc3892518ae" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "h=\\frac{1}{2A\\pi r}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dd8c229b-04e9-5db8-b9b0-7619b9b60f94" :answer/id #uuid "dd8c229b-04e9-5db8-b9b0-7619b9b60f94" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "h=\\frac{2A}{\\pi r}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b984c89f-22b6-5f39-a9e4-709f228a20bf" :answer/id #uuid "b984c89f-22b6-5f39-a9e4-709f228a20bf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "h=\\frac{A}{2\\pi r}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-509ef183-713a-5b80-9cea-d36045a8b4db" :answer/id #uuid "509ef183-713a-5b80-9cea-d36045a8b4db" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "h=\\frac{A}{2\\pi }" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5f309670-2000-5f40-9e63-064e5228ef92" :answer/id #uuid "5f309670-2000-5f40-9e63-064e5228ef92" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "h=\\frac{A}{\\pi r}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-de5d5814-9f69-514d-a764-4ee1edcf544a" :question/id #uuid "de5d5814-9f69-514d-a764-4ee1edcf544a" :question/math-academy-id "q-215396" :question/problem "If $2xz-3y=4w,$ then $y=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We need to isolate $y,$ so we will treat the other variables $w,x,$ and $z$ as constants. We can use the addition and multiplication principles to solve for $y.$\n\nFirst, we subtract $2xz$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2xz-3y & =4w \\\\ 2xz-3y-2xz & =4w-2xz \\\\ -3y & =4w-2xz\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide by $-3$ to get $y$ alone:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3y & =4w-2xz \\\\ \\frac{-3y}{-3} & =\\frac{4w-2xz}{-3} \\\\ y & =\\frac{4w-2xz}{-3} \\\\ y & =\\frac{2xz-4w}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-2ef1b194-3723-50a3-b24b-91683d8db081"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-2ef1b194-3723-50a3-b24b-91683d8db081" :answer-field/id #uuid "2ef1b194-3723-50a3-b24b-91683d8db081" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-79c8330d-6dc6-557a-9815-0dc1258b5bc1" :answer-field/choices ["answer-79c8330d-6dc6-557a-9815-0dc1258b5bc1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-79c8330d-6dc6-557a-9815-0dc1258b5bc1" :answer/id #uuid "79c8330d-6dc6-557a-9815-0dc1258b5bc1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2xz-4w}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-220b7284-89df-58d8-b7fb-14c6a632a4f4" :question/id #uuid "220b7284-89df-58d8-b7fb-14c6a632a4f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-44850" :question/problem "In business, the economic profit is defined as\n\n$$\nE=PQ-C,\n$$\n\nwhere $P$ is the sale price of a good, $Q$ is the number of goods sold, and $C$ is the cost of producing $Q$ goods. \n\nWhat is the formula for the price in terms of the other variables?" :question/worked-solution "We need to isolate $P,$ so we will treat the other variables $E,$ $Q,$ and $C$ as constants. We can use the addition and multiplication principles to solve for $P.$\n\nFirst, we add $C$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E & =PQ-C \\\\ E+C & =PQ-C+C \\\\ E+C & =PQ\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide by $Q$ to get $P$ alone:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E+C & =PQ \\\\ \\frac{E+C}{Q} & =\\frac{PQ}{Q} \\\\ \\frac{E+C}{Q} & =P\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $P=\\frac{E+C}{Q}.$\n\n**Note:** This result assumes that $Q\\ne 0.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7599f77d-da1f-5c75-baeb-b3ad971719bd"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7599f77d-da1f-5c75-baeb-b3ad971719bd" :answer-field/id #uuid "7599f77d-da1f-5c75-baeb-b3ad971719bd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-65f5c1ef-d90e-568c-bb69-d1d84bbf05ac" :answer-field/choices ["answer-0776833c-e0c2-532d-b051-2d003c8e095a" "answer-39705b79-f9e1-5882-96bb-64867d45e168" "answer-65f5c1ef-d90e-568c-bb69-d1d84bbf05ac" "answer-b9a6184b-cc9c-5368-98bb-fcc102c67792" "answer-c8133753-993a-528a-abab-a54fb3d9628e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0776833c-e0c2-532d-b051-2d003c8e095a" :answer/id #uuid "0776833c-e0c2-532d-b051-2d003c8e095a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "C=PQ-E" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-39705b79-f9e1-5882-96bb-64867d45e168" :answer/id #uuid "39705b79-f9e1-5882-96bb-64867d45e168" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "P=Q(E+C)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-65f5c1ef-d90e-568c-bb69-d1d84bbf05ac" :answer/id #uuid "65f5c1ef-d90e-568c-bb69-d1d84bbf05ac" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "P=\\frac{E+C}{Q}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b9a6184b-cc9c-5368-98bb-fcc102c67792" :answer/id #uuid "b9a6184b-cc9c-5368-98bb-fcc102c67792" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "P=\\frac{E-C}{Q}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c8133753-993a-528a-abab-a54fb3d9628e" :answer/id #uuid "c8133753-993a-528a-abab-a54fb3d9628e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Q=\\frac{E+C}{P}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186065434 :tutorial/content "Sometimes we might be given an equation that contains more than one variable and asked to isolate one of the variables. This means that we need to put the variable we're interested in on one side of the equation (on its own) and everything else on the other.\n\nFor example, suppose we want to isolate $n$ in the equation\n\n$$\n2n - k = q + 5k.\n$$\n\nThe equation has three variables, $n,$ $k,$ and $q.$ However, we will treat the variables $k$ and $q$ as constants because we are solving for $n$ only. We can use the addition and multiplication principles to solve for $n\\mathbin{.}$ \n\nFirst, we add $k$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t2n - k &= q + 5k \\[5pt]\n\t\t2n - k + k &= q + 5k +k \\[5pt]\n\t\t2n &= q + 6k \\\n\t}\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, we divide both sides of the equation by $2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t2n &= q + 6k \\[5pt]\n\t\t\\dfrac {2n} {2} &= \\dfrac {q+6k} {2} \\\nn &= \\dfrac {q+6k} {2}\n\t}\n$$\n\n \n\nBecause the equation is now solved for $n$, we say that we've made $n$ **the subject** of the equation." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064443 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186131497 :answer-field/choices 17592186131498] [:db/retract 17592186131497 :answer-field/choices 17592186131499] [:db/retract 17592186131497 :answer-field/choices 17592186131500] [:db/retract 17592186131497 :answer-field/choices 17592186131501] [:db/retract 17592186131497 :answer-field/choices 17592186131502] {:db/id 17592186134243 :question/problem "Find the orthogonal projection of the vector $x$ onto the plane $r=sv+tw,$ where $s,t\\in R,$ and\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $v$ and $w$ are linearly independent. \n\nThe orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nSince our plane passes through the origin, it defines the subspace $S=\\text{Span}\\{v,w\\}$ in ${R}^{3}.$ In our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ v & w \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}3 & 7 \\\\ 7 & 19\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 28\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{8}[\\begin{aligned}19 & -7 \\\\ -7 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 28\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186131497 :answer-field/correct "answer-a33bde79-ffb2-5dbe-bf12-ffd8a5fc10a4" :answer-field/choices ["answer-908d17a2-f2f1-56f9-acd8-74a95eb4695f" "answer-db1a839b-51b6-5277-a632-d4ded0706f14" "answer-8d1837d2-279c-5b39-b335-7e4e449a5f14" "answer-a33bde79-ffb2-5dbe-bf12-ffd8a5fc10a4" "answer-23c5f794-99b0-5846-9311-18d5de992953"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-908d17a2-f2f1-56f9-acd8-74a95eb4695f" :answer/id #uuid "908d17a2-f2f1-56f9-acd8-74a95eb4695f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-db1a839b-51b6-5277-a632-d4ded0706f14" :answer/id #uuid "db1a839b-51b6-5277-a632-d4ded0706f14" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8d1837d2-279c-5b39-b335-7e4e449a5f14" :answer/id #uuid "8d1837d2-279c-5b39-b335-7e4e449a5f14" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a33bde79-ffb2-5dbe-bf12-ffd8a5fc10a4" :answer/id #uuid "a33bde79-ffb2-5dbe-bf12-ffd8a5fc10a4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-23c5f794-99b0-5846-9311-18d5de992953" :answer/id #uuid "23c5f794-99b0-5846-9311-18d5de992953" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186133788 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-86358ef9-78e9-5caa-a7a3-a7c614e96d69" "question-e47080dd-98d4-5498-967c-b6d64e7e3867"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-86358ef9-78e9-5caa-a7a3-a7c614e96d69" :question/id #uuid "86358ef9-78e9-5caa-a7a3-a7c614e96d69" :question/math-academy-id "q-40628" :question/problem "Verify whether the vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}$ are linearly independent, and find the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the vectors $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we verify whether ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}$ are linearly independent. So, we start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-2{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -3\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, the pivot columns correspond to the linearly independent vectors ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ that form the basis of our span.\n\nNow, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the linearly independent columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,⇒\\,{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}6 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}15 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}15 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5ae069ad-82b0-5089-9f78-df2bcd3c3331"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5ae069ad-82b0-5089-9f78-df2bcd3c3331" :answer-field/id #uuid "5ae069ad-82b0-5089-9f78-df2bcd3c3331" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-69d00f66-bac7-5a07-842c-86c8ae454700" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2f402a3a-4f08-5c72-a337-37b1173065e9" "answer-924789e2-65b9-582c-b2a0-b829c5928e12" "answer-69d00f66-bac7-5a07-842c-86c8ae454700" "answer-94182863-ea1a-5c03-b764-976abf2d1657" "answer-795fe3b9-08f5-5072-9238-46b50ec1f87a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2f402a3a-4f08-5c72-a337-37b1173065e9" :answer/id #uuid "2f402a3a-4f08-5c72-a337-37b1173065e9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-924789e2-65b9-582c-b2a0-b829c5928e12" :answer/id #uuid "924789e2-65b9-582c-b2a0-b829c5928e12" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-69d00f66-bac7-5a07-842c-86c8ae454700" :answer/id #uuid "69d00f66-bac7-5a07-842c-86c8ae454700" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-94182863-ea1a-5c03-b764-976abf2d1657" :answer/id #uuid "94182863-ea1a-5c03-b764-976abf2d1657" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-795fe3b9-08f5-5072-9238-46b50ec1f87a" :answer/id #uuid "795fe3b9-08f5-5072-9238-46b50ec1f87a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-e47080dd-98d4-5498-967c-b6d64e7e3867" :question/id #uuid "e47080dd-98d4-5498-967c-b6d64e7e3867" :question/math-academy-id "q-40629" :question/problem "Verify whether the vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}$ are linearly independent, and find the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the vectors $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we verify whether ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}$ are linearly independent. So, we start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & [\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}↔{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, the pivot columns correspond to the linearly independent vectors ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ that form the basis of our span.\n\nNow, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the linearly independent columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,⇒\\,{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}9 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b0ad7dc8-4543-5cca-8939-06979b0f56c1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b0ad7dc8-4543-5cca-8939-06979b0f56c1" :answer-field/id #uuid "b0ad7dc8-4543-5cca-8939-06979b0f56c1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-2e54bdc8-061f-5aca-ad4c-768aadfc8c96" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e1378868-2a9d-52dd-8e6a-ac7feee66d57" "answer-2e54bdc8-061f-5aca-ad4c-768aadfc8c96" "answer-2d911574-ccd3-5c98-bd89-40abf5381b05" "answer-f00d63d7-b728-5541-b6c4-ed9e9411144d" "answer-96ca6b9c-ffc7-5276-bee4-ff0be2c4198c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e1378868-2a9d-52dd-8e6a-ac7feee66d57" :answer/id #uuid "e1378868-2a9d-52dd-8e6a-ac7feee66d57" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2e54bdc8-061f-5aca-ad4c-768aadfc8c96" :answer/id #uuid "2e54bdc8-061f-5aca-ad4c-768aadfc8c96" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2d911574-ccd3-5c98-bd89-40abf5381b05" :answer/id #uuid "2d911574-ccd3-5c98-bd89-40abf5381b05" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f00d63d7-b728-5541-b6c4-ed9e9411144d" :answer/id #uuid "f00d63d7-b728-5541-b6c4-ed9e9411144d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-96ca6b9c-ffc7-5276-bee4-ff0be2c4198c" :answer/id #uuid "96ca6b9c-ffc7-5276-bee4-ff0be2c4198c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186394738 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $v$ and $w$ are linearly independent. \n\nThe orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nSince our plane passes through the origin, it defines the subspace $S=\\text{Span}\\{v,w\\}$ in ${R}^{3}.$ In our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ v & w \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & \\,\\,-2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & \\,\\,-2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & \\,\\,-2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & \\,\\,-2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}11 & -5 \\\\ -5 & 9\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & \\,\\,-2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{74}[\\begin{aligned}9 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 11\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & \\,\\,-2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186388967 :question/problem "Find the vector in the subspace spanned by $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ \nthat is closest to the vector $x,$ where \n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are linearly independent. \n\nThe closest vector to $x$ in the subspace spanned by $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace, which is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}10 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{26}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 10\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "activity-a5e50e5c-f6d4-570d-8bfd-dd50d0a5e6dd" :activity/id #uuid "a5e50e5c-f6d4-570d-8bfd-dd50d0a5e6dd" :activity/title "Projecting Vectors Onto Subspaces in Euclidean Spaces (Arbitrary Bases)" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186049970 :activity/course 17592186046658 :activity/steps ["step-772f1302-aeb0-5063-a912-a0f326d01ac2" "step-ad4ab2c8-0a51-5f88-9dd3-02a2bc3345a4" "step-55b0eb69-ee74-5a65-98a4-635c7ba90856" "step-eff9b1cb-cb04-5ebf-80e3-b9dafaf06cbe" "step-8bf7a2d1-6483-574a-bd3f-c2612cb3dafd"] :activity/first-step "step-772f1302-aeb0-5063-a912-a0f326d01ac2" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-772f1302-aeb0-5063-a912-a0f326d01ac2" :step/id #uuid "772f1302-aeb0-5063-a912-a0f326d01ac2" :step/content 17592186134243 :step/next "step-ad4ab2c8-0a51-5f88-9dd3-02a2bc3345a4" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-ad4ab2c8-0a51-5f88-9dd3-02a2bc3345a4" :step/id #uuid "ad4ab2c8-0a51-5f88-9dd3-02a2bc3345a4" :step/content "question-86358ef9-78e9-5caa-a7a3-a7c614e96d69" :step/next "step-55b0eb69-ee74-5a65-98a4-635c7ba90856" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-55b0eb69-ee74-5a65-98a4-635c7ba90856" :step/id #uuid "55b0eb69-ee74-5a65-98a4-635c7ba90856" :step/content "question-e47080dd-98d4-5498-967c-b6d64e7e3867" :step/next "step-eff9b1cb-cb04-5ebf-80e3-b9dafaf06cbe" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-eff9b1cb-cb04-5ebf-80e3-b9dafaf06cbe" :step/id #uuid "eff9b1cb-cb04-5ebf-80e3-b9dafaf06cbe" :step/content 17592186394738 :step/next "step-8bf7a2d1-6483-574a-bd3f-c2612cb3dafd" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-8bf7a2d1-6483-574a-bd3f-c2612cb3dafd" :step/id #uuid "8bf7a2d1-6483-574a-bd3f-c2612cb3dafd" :step/content 17592186388967 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186068680 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-a9ba7727-634b-5b27-90cd-2da161027238" "question-ce23a05a-85d4-5790-9eda-0cc10c821050"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-a9ba7727-634b-5b27-90cd-2da161027238" :question/id #uuid "a9ba7727-634b-5b27-90cd-2da161027238" :question/math-academy-id "q-3710" :question/problem "![](images/66/663096ab9ecc1daca22587d94526cbf0db841602ca0b64eaa1a9477ec8a35aee.png)\n\nWhat is the length of $a$ in the triangle shown above?" :question/worked-solution "Here, we know the lengths of the hypotenuse and one leg, so let $b=12$ and $c=13.$ We need to find $a,$ the length of the other leg.\n\nAccording to the Pythagorean theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}^{2} & ={a}^{2}+{b}^{2} \\\\ {a}^{2} & ={c}^{2}-{b}^{2} \\\\ a & =\\sqrt{{c}^{2}-{b}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in the values for $b$ and $c$ gives us\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\sqrt{(13)^{2}-(12)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{169-144} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-912e2818-58ec-5b3c-9288-7b141fa8ef9b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-912e2818-58ec-5b3c-9288-7b141fa8ef9b" :answer-field/id #uuid "912e2818-58ec-5b3c-9288-7b141fa8ef9b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-05f4b190-6978-5583-8c36-6d2df2e0ac80" :answer-field/choices ["answer-0f2d72d2-a8d1-5dd9-8bea-42b1a2417a31" "answer-03528b79-9066-5700-95cd-a28d264f9a9b" "answer-6291ad98-e54f-5119-bf5f-b501c0c71255" "answer-05f4b190-6978-5583-8c36-6d2df2e0ac80" "answer-9b15c0f3-e9b3-5a77-9b5d-950decf55d35"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0f2d72d2-a8d1-5dd9-8bea-42b1a2417a31" :answer/id #uuid "0f2d72d2-a8d1-5dd9-8bea-42b1a2417a31" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-03528b79-9066-5700-95cd-a28d264f9a9b" :answer/id #uuid "03528b79-9066-5700-95cd-a28d264f9a9b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "11" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6291ad98-e54f-5119-bf5f-b501c0c71255" :answer/id #uuid "6291ad98-e54f-5119-bf5f-b501c0c71255" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-05f4b190-6978-5583-8c36-6d2df2e0ac80" :answer/id #uuid "05f4b190-6978-5583-8c36-6d2df2e0ac80" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9b15c0f3-e9b3-5a77-9b5d-950decf55d35" :answer/id #uuid "9b15c0f3-e9b3-5a77-9b5d-950decf55d35" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-ce23a05a-85d4-5790-9eda-0cc10c821050" :question/id #uuid "ce23a05a-85d4-5790-9eda-0cc10c821050" :question/math-academy-id "q-5650" :question/problem "![](images/3c/3c5b5a5c9531b6568c78633f5c44c8332cc722c9d88860a4b6bb9c2c336a1683.png)\n\nWhat is the length of $a$ in the triangle shown above?" :question/worked-solution "Here, we know the lengths of the hypotenuse and one leg, so let $b=\\frac{15}{2}$ and $c=\\frac{39}{2}.$ We need to find $a,$ the length of the other leg.\n\nAccording to the Pythagorean theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}^{2} & ={a}^{2}+{b}^{2} \\\\ {a}^{2} & ={c}^{2}-{b}^{2} \\\\ a & =\\sqrt{{c}^{2}-{b}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in the values for $b$ and $c$ gives us\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{39}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{15}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1\\,521}{4}-\\frac{225}{4}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{324} \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-8a817628-1149-5ae9-912f-5d3dd1012acd"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-8a817628-1149-5ae9-912f-5d3dd1012acd" :answer-field/id #uuid "8a817628-1149-5ae9-912f-5d3dd1012acd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-79751c18-eaa3-5051-9226-7e74700d3de0" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7c96d7b0-33e5-5715-9230-bacb2c9fa936" "answer-79751c18-eaa3-5051-9226-7e74700d3de0" "answer-3a9cdac2-f621-564f-8709-0203bdf85dd6" "answer-ac564bf3-c6f3-5221-811b-35794c5d512f" "answer-60d53825-bf07-5091-90ed-8b078be85249"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7c96d7b0-33e5-5715-9230-bacb2c9fa936" :answer/id #uuid "7c96d7b0-33e5-5715-9230-bacb2c9fa936" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "17" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-79751c18-eaa3-5051-9226-7e74700d3de0" :answer/id #uuid "79751c18-eaa3-5051-9226-7e74700d3de0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "18" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3a9cdac2-f621-564f-8709-0203bdf85dd6" :answer/id #uuid "3a9cdac2-f621-564f-8709-0203bdf85dd6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{35}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ac564bf3-c6f3-5221-811b-35794c5d512f" :answer/id #uuid "ac564bf3-c6f3-5221-811b-35794c5d512f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{323}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-60d53825-bf07-5091-90ed-8b078be85249" :answer/id #uuid "60d53825-bf07-5091-90ed-8b078be85249" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{873}{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186068679 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-0d67eb70-42fa-5852-8a29-377a03af6acb"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-0d67eb70-42fa-5852-8a29-377a03af6acb" :question/id #uuid "0d67eb70-42fa-5852-8a29-377a03af6acb" :question/math-academy-id "q-82392" :question/problem "A right triangle has legs of length $4$ and $8.$ Calculate the length of its hypotenuse." :question/worked-solution "Let $a=4,$ $b=8,$ and $c$ be the length of the hypotenuse. According to the Pythagorean theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}^{2} & ={a}^{2}+{b}^{2} \\\\ c & =\\sqrt{{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in the values for $a$ and $b$ gives us\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\sqrt{(4)^{2}+(8)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+64} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{80} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d1d1d5a9-6a7c-5386-ac49-57029f04b961"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d1d1d5a9-6a7c-5386-ac49-57029f04b961" :answer-field/id #uuid "d1d1d5a9-6a7c-5386-ac49-57029f04b961" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-44486489-16d0-571b-ac64-959574132778" :answer-field/choices ["answer-89a36b84-72a1-56e6-b785-03e44768abb4" "answer-fb25e632-b168-5065-ad33-00818427e651" "answer-2ae35f5d-fc84-5280-980e-ba21def821dc" "answer-44486489-16d0-571b-ac64-959574132778" "answer-f2f259dd-0c52-5cac-9647-ed39eaed1dc3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-89a36b84-72a1-56e6-b785-03e44768abb4" :answer/id #uuid "89a36b84-72a1-56e6-b785-03e44768abb4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fb25e632-b168-5065-ad33-00818427e651" :answer/id #uuid "fb25e632-b168-5065-ad33-00818427e651" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2ae35f5d-fc84-5280-980e-ba21def821dc" :answer/id #uuid "2ae35f5d-fc84-5280-980e-ba21def821dc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\sqrt{10}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-44486489-16d0-571b-ac64-959574132778" :answer/id #uuid "44486489-16d0-571b-ac64-959574132778" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\sqrt{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f2f259dd-0c52-5cac-9647-ed39eaed1dc3" :answer/id #uuid "f2f259dd-0c52-5cac-9647-ed39eaed1dc3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7\\sqrt{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-5d7d6b48-4082-54df-a38c-264067bb8314" :activity/id #uuid "5d7d6b48-4082-54df-a38c-264067bb8314" :activity/title "The Pythagorean Theorem" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186049224 :activity/course 17592186046648 :activity/steps ["step-e100fe69-e3de-5f66-8bf1-1a8103c6fd5e" "step-8c98aac9-e3f2-5011-8aaa-b9b56e695d7d" "step-a571d258-25ad-5505-b4a6-44cb1f7c2043"] :activity/first-step "step-e100fe69-e3de-5f66-8bf1-1a8103c6fd5e" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-e100fe69-e3de-5f66-8bf1-1a8103c6fd5e" :step/id #uuid "e100fe69-e3de-5f66-8bf1-1a8103c6fd5e" :step/content "question-a9ba7727-634b-5b27-90cd-2da161027238" :step/next "step-8c98aac9-e3f2-5011-8aaa-b9b56e695d7d" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-8c98aac9-e3f2-5011-8aaa-b9b56e695d7d" :step/id #uuid "8c98aac9-e3f2-5011-8aaa-b9b56e695d7d" :step/content "question-ce23a05a-85d4-5790-9eda-0cc10c821050" :step/next "step-a571d258-25ad-5505-b4a6-44cb1f7c2043" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-a571d258-25ad-5505-b4a6-44cb1f7c2043" :step/id #uuid "a571d258-25ad-5505-b4a6-44cb1f7c2043" :step/content "question-0d67eb70-42fa-5852-8a29-377a03af6acb" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186179913 :question/answer-fields 17592186177147] [:db/retract 17592186180096 :question/answer-fields 17592186178245] {:db/id 17592186179692 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-5208d74a-f4b7-5436-9bf1-918aa4bcaa25" "question-bf4a1221-96ce-539b-bfe1-3711516162e7"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186179693 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-f72c0da0-a5f4-5e3b-8c36-5827ed531170" "question-e3dde6ce-080b-590e-80f0-4c60a929946f" "question-2593d620-c288-5ff9-965a-23a30b035fd9"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186179715 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-5fda4f28-bb84-514e-9e1c-0feb53f5b922"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-5208d74a-f4b7-5436-9bf1-918aa4bcaa25" :question/id #uuid "5208d74a-f4b7-5436-9bf1-918aa4bcaa25" :question/math-academy-id "q-163585" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis. When the displacement of $P$ from the origin is $x\\,m,$ the velocity is $v\\,m/s,$ and the acceleration is $4x\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ If $P$ has velocity $2\\sqrt{3}\\,m/s$ when its displacement is $2\\sqrt{2}\\,m,$ find the values of $x$ for which $P$ is instantaneously at rest." :question/worked-solution "![](images/01/017463f3ac8abac9c2b00c4d9a715a4109a5f4bc1b070e0a12c4d3a7bac64351.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $4x\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=4x.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=4x.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x=\\int 4x\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=2{x}^{2}+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=2\\sqrt{3}\\,m/s$ when $x=2\\sqrt{2}\\,m,$ we can find the value of the constant $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =2{x}^{2}+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(2\\sqrt{3})^{2} & =2(2\\sqrt{2})^{2}+C \\\\ C & =6-16 \\\\ C & =-10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=2{x}^{2}-10.\n$$\n\nFinally, the particle is instantaneously at rest when $v(x)=0\\,m/s.$ We find the displacement of the particle when instantaneously at rest by substituting $v=0$ in the equation above and solving for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =2{x}^{2}-10 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(0)^{2} & =2{x}^{2}-10 \\\\ 2{x}^{2} & =10 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =5 \\\\ x & =\\pm \\sqrt{5}\\,m\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-748a88b6-3bf6-5b57-aacf-5ce371f8acba"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-748a88b6-3bf6-5b57-aacf-5ce371f8acba" :answer-field/id #uuid "748a88b6-3bf6-5b57-aacf-5ce371f8acba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-acbb534e-c48b-5374-b024-0f4966f54ecd" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f502dce3-880c-5ad9-9d68-43c719b39205" "answer-6cf5d513-6835-539d-a925-f3dbf75e5b3d" "answer-7b71dd8f-bac1-5c10-8567-8f83b147a02c" "answer-acbb534e-c48b-5374-b024-0f4966f54ecd" "answer-1499bde2-2ab6-5730-a6dc-62d7a289a0d9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f502dce3-880c-5ad9-9d68-43c719b39205" :answer/id #uuid "f502dce3-880c-5ad9-9d68-43c719b39205" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=5\\,m" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6cf5d513-6835-539d-a925-f3dbf75e5b3d" :answer/id #uuid "6cf5d513-6835-539d-a925-f3dbf75e5b3d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=\\pm 2\\,m" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7b71dd8f-bac1-5c10-8567-8f83b147a02c" :answer/id #uuid "7b71dd8f-bac1-5c10-8567-8f83b147a02c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=\\pm 3\\sqrt{2}\\,m" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-acbb534e-c48b-5374-b024-0f4966f54ecd" :answer/id #uuid "acbb534e-c48b-5374-b024-0f4966f54ecd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=\\pm \\sqrt{5}\\,m" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1499bde2-2ab6-5730-a6dc-62d7a289a0d9" :answer/id #uuid "1499bde2-2ab6-5730-a6dc-62d7a289a0d9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=\\sqrt{3}\\,m" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-bf4a1221-96ce-539b-bfe1-3711516162e7" :question/id #uuid "bf4a1221-96ce-539b-bfe1-3711516162e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-163580" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis. When the displacement of $P$ from the origin is $x\\,m,$ the velocity is $v\\,m/s,$ and the acceleration is $(2x-3)\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ If $P$ has velocity $2\\,m/s$ when its displacement is $3\\,m,$ find the values of $x$ for which $P$ is instantaneously at rest." :question/worked-solution "![](images/ca/ca6267f534b9cad9bc1646eef473d85aab1ba6a5f4738b8212b107b753ea2381.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $(2x-3)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=2x-3.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=2x-3.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x=\\int (2x-3)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}={x}^{2}-3x+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=2\\,m/s$ when $x=3\\,m,$ we can find the value of the constant $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & ={x}^{2}-3x+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(2)^{2} & =(3)^{2}-3(3)+C \\\\ C & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}={x}^{2}-3x+2.\n$$\n\nFinally, the particle is instantaneously at rest when $v(x)=0\\,m/s.$ We find the displacement of the particle when instantaneously at rest by substituting $v=0$ in the equation above and solving for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & ={x}^{2}-3x+2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(0)^{2} & ={x}^{2}-3x+2 \\\\ {x}^{2}-3x+2 & =0 \\\\ (x-1)(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =1\\,m,\\,2\\,m.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c6154e74-64bf-5093-bf56-6eed3e0fc3f3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c6154e74-64bf-5093-bf56-6eed3e0fc3f3" :answer-field/id #uuid "c6154e74-64bf-5093-bf56-6eed3e0fc3f3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-a0e376fc-17af-5815-b320-dee9d3fc6efe" :answer-field/choices ["answer-aadde328-0a84-557d-ab82-907dd5467e85" "answer-b99f81d8-cedc-552f-8e21-52c06f6a9822" "answer-69d48d35-eeeb-5306-96d0-da006a058aed" "answer-a0e376fc-17af-5815-b320-dee9d3fc6efe" "answer-c561cd7c-8f01-53ea-a88e-1356c8d77c5e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-aadde328-0a84-557d-ab82-907dd5467e85" :answer/id #uuid "aadde328-0a84-557d-ab82-907dd5467e85" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=1\\,m" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b99f81d8-cedc-552f-8e21-52c06f6a9822" :answer/id #uuid "b99f81d8-cedc-552f-8e21-52c06f6a9822" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=4\\,m" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-69d48d35-eeeb-5306-96d0-da006a058aed" :answer/id #uuid "69d48d35-eeeb-5306-96d0-da006a058aed" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=5\\,m" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a0e376fc-17af-5815-b320-dee9d3fc6efe" :answer/id #uuid "a0e376fc-17af-5815-b320-dee9d3fc6efe" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x=1\\,m$ and $x=2\\,m$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c561cd7c-8f01-53ea-a88e-1356c8d77c5e" :answer/id #uuid "c561cd7c-8f01-53ea-a88e-1356c8d77c5e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x=2\\,m$ and $x=5\\,m$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-f72c0da0-a5f4-5e3b-8c36-5827ed531170" :question/id #uuid "f72c0da0-a5f4-5e3b-8c36-5827ed531170" :question/math-academy-id "q-163556" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis. When the displacement of $P$ from the origin is $x\\,m,$ the velocity is $v\\,m/s,$ and the acceleration is $(3+kx)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ where $k$ is a positive constant. If $P$ has velocity $v=8\\,m/s$ at the origin and the particle has velocity $v=8\\sqrt{2}\\,m/s$ when its displacement is $8\\,m,$ find the value of $k.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/f3/f368d2de12462a1790322edca188232bf8fafe3951facdb29b334e285807b9fe.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $(3+kx)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=3+kx.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=3+kx.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x=\\int (3+kx)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=3x+\\frac{k{x}^{2}}{2}+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=8\\,m/s$ when $x=0\\,m,$ we can find the value of the constant $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =3x+\\frac{k{x}^{2}}{2}+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(8)^{2} & =3(0)+\\frac{k(0)^{2}}{2}+C \\\\ C & =32\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=3x+\\frac{k{x}^{2}}{2}+32.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $v=8\\sqrt{2}\\,m/s$ when $x=8\\,m,$ we can find the value of the positive constant $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =3x+\\frac{k{x}^{2}}{2}+32 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(8\\sqrt{2})^{2} & =3(8)+\\frac{k(8)^{2}}{2}+32 \\\\ 64 & =24+32k+32 \\\\ 32k & =8 \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-6c0cd56a-fac1-59c9-9082-8dcbbdf2dead"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-6c0cd56a-fac1-59c9-9082-8dcbbdf2dead" :answer-field/id #uuid "6c0cd56a-fac1-59c9-9082-8dcbbdf2dead" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-93f5f517-1af6-5399-a4d2-94637bc4642f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-77c1c959-8901-5fd3-bde1-0be7a13d7921" "answer-d60b1661-414b-525a-a604-03b55bb6095a" "answer-93f5f517-1af6-5399-a4d2-94637bc4642f" "answer-a61b1f2d-1d39-5240-aeb9-34d945752a5c" "answer-1ad4a456-d983-59d9-aa10-433b9a7fb149"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-77c1c959-8901-5fd3-bde1-0be7a13d7921" :answer/id #uuid "77c1c959-8901-5fd3-bde1-0be7a13d7921" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d60b1661-414b-525a-a604-03b55bb6095a" :answer/id #uuid "d60b1661-414b-525a-a604-03b55bb6095a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-93f5f517-1af6-5399-a4d2-94637bc4642f" :answer/id #uuid "93f5f517-1af6-5399-a4d2-94637bc4642f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a61b1f2d-1d39-5240-aeb9-34d945752a5c" :answer/id #uuid "a61b1f2d-1d39-5240-aeb9-34d945752a5c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1ad4a456-d983-59d9-aa10-433b9a7fb149" :answer/id #uuid "1ad4a456-d983-59d9-aa10-433b9a7fb149" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-e3dde6ce-080b-590e-80f0-4c60a929946f" :question/id #uuid "e3dde6ce-080b-590e-80f0-4c60a929946f" :question/math-academy-id "q-163547" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis. When the displacement of $P$ from the origin is $x\\,m,$ the velocity is $v\\,m/s,$ the magnitude of the acceleration is $k{x}^{3}\\,{m/s}^{2},$ where $k$ is a positive constant, and the acceleration is directed toward $O.$ If $P$ has velocity $v=8\\sqrt{17}\\,m/s$ at the origin and the particle has a velocity of $v=8\\,m/s$ when its displacement is $4\\,m,$ find the value of $k.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/f3/f3879ccc6084d9a0ef8bef2e211e2e7fd9dcc811fb04f06c2ef7429e4d574871.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ has magnitude $k{x}^{3}\\,{m/s}^{2}$ and is directed *toward* $O,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-k{x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=-k{x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x=\\int (-k{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=-\\frac{k}{4}{x}^{4}+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=8\\sqrt{17}\\,m/s$ when $x=0\\,m,$ we can find the value of the constant $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =-\\frac{k}{4}{x}^{4}+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(8\\sqrt{17})^{2} & =-\\frac{k}{4}(0)^{4}+C \\\\ C & =544\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=544-\\frac{k}{4}{x}^{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $v=8\\,m/s$ when $x=4\\,m,$ we can find the value of the positive constant $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =544-\\frac{k}{4}{x}^{4} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(8)^{2} & =544-\\frac{k}{4}(4)^{4} \\\\ 32 & =544-64k \\\\ 64k & =512 \\\\ k & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-efd9e216-e266-5141-b8bb-3d95bb41fe42"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-efd9e216-e266-5141-b8bb-3d95bb41fe42" :answer-field/id #uuid "efd9e216-e266-5141-b8bb-3d95bb41fe42" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-e8f48f14-0682-5d40-a4ae-8e91cb638dd9" :answer-field/choices ["answer-0118c6ec-baa3-5b9a-b5f4-b5775a6bc23d" "answer-f606240c-a0f3-5b26-a8ff-817bf6b69d1b" "answer-e8f48f14-0682-5d40-a4ae-8e91cb638dd9" "answer-0136609f-e6ce-547c-b8ef-b3df6212d44a" "answer-0c8bd597-16e0-5127-bbc8-297250b46ed1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0118c6ec-baa3-5b9a-b5f4-b5775a6bc23d" :answer/id #uuid "0118c6ec-baa3-5b9a-b5f4-b5775a6bc23d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f606240c-a0f3-5b26-a8ff-817bf6b69d1b" :answer/id #uuid "f606240c-a0f3-5b26-a8ff-817bf6b69d1b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e8f48f14-0682-5d40-a4ae-8e91cb638dd9" :answer/id #uuid "e8f48f14-0682-5d40-a4ae-8e91cb638dd9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0136609f-e6ce-547c-b8ef-b3df6212d44a" :answer/id #uuid "0136609f-e6ce-547c-b8ef-b3df6212d44a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0c8bd597-16e0-5127-bbc8-297250b46ed1" :answer/id #uuid "0c8bd597-16e0-5127-bbc8-297250b46ed1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-2593d620-c288-5ff9-965a-23a30b035fd9" :question/id #uuid "2593d620-c288-5ff9-965a-23a30b035fd9" :question/math-academy-id "q-163498" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the positive $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin is $x\\,m,$ the velocity is $v\\,m/s,$ and the acceleration is $k\\sqrt[3]{x}\\,{m/s}^{2},$ where $k$ is a positive constant. If $P$ has velocity $v=2\\,m/s$ at the origin and the particle has velocity $v=8\\,m/s$ when its displacement is $8\\,m,$ find the value of $k.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/77/771929267e75b5d45b9777399c784fafc570386834eee6738fead9ff1bb78e5e.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $k\\sqrt[3]{x}\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=k\\sqrt[3]{x}.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=k\\sqrt[3]{x}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x=\\int k\\sqrt[3]{x}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=\\frac{3k}{4}{x}^{4/3}+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=2\\,m/s$ when $x=0\\,m,$ we can find the value of the constant $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{3k}{4}{x}^{4/3}+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(2)^{2} & =\\frac{3k}{4}(0)^{4/3}+C \\\\ C & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=\\frac{3k}{4}{x}^{4/3}+2.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $v=8\\,m/s$ when $x=8\\,m,$ we can find the value of the positive constant $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{3k}{4}{x}^{4/3}+2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(8)^{2} & =\\frac{3k}{4}(8)^{4/3}+2 \\\\ 32 & =12k+2 \\\\ 12k & =30 \\\\ k & =\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c645bc4d-e925-5887-895a-ddac573c52dd"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c645bc4d-e925-5887-895a-ddac573c52dd" :answer-field/id #uuid "c645bc4d-e925-5887-895a-ddac573c52dd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-5034dbdd-3931-5511-b9be-2d0348496992" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b7073903-8876-5da4-a63c-6a4a53306e98" "answer-ea6617f6-27a7-5521-aa41-9e2ac702febc" "answer-24057b29-196d-561b-b6d6-0653368bc625" "answer-5034dbdd-3931-5511-b9be-2d0348496992" "answer-7e7c10d2-5b64-52e8-b62a-9f3237aa49b0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b7073903-8876-5da4-a63c-6a4a53306e98" :answer/id #uuid "b7073903-8876-5da4-a63c-6a4a53306e98" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ea6617f6-27a7-5521-aa41-9e2ac702febc" :answer/id #uuid "ea6617f6-27a7-5521-aa41-9e2ac702febc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-24057b29-196d-561b-b6d6-0653368bc625" :answer/id #uuid "24057b29-196d-561b-b6d6-0653368bc625" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5034dbdd-3931-5511-b9be-2d0348496992" :answer/id #uuid "5034dbdd-3931-5511-b9be-2d0348496992" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7e7c10d2-5b64-52e8-b62a-9f3237aa49b0" :answer/id #uuid "7e7c10d2-5b64-52e8-b62a-9f3237aa49b0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-5fda4f28-bb84-514e-9e1c-0feb53f5b922" :question/id #uuid "5fda4f28-bb84-514e-9e1c-0feb53f5b922" :question/math-academy-id "q-163554" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x$ where $x\\ge 0.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin is $x\\,m,$ the velocity is $v\\,m/s,$ and the acceleration is $(7-x)\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ If $P$ has velocity $v=\\sqrt{6}\\,m/s$ at the origin, find the maximum velocity of the particle." :question/worked-solution "![](images/51/51ccc6e3e341931c7a244c4617ede78f901478487589f9023b16c3dae80ebc62.png)\n\nThe extrema of the velocity occur at the critical points of $v.$ Since the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $(7-x)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=7-x.\n$$\n\nTo find the moments where $v$ is a maximum, we need to solve $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=0$ and test each solution using the first or second derivative test.\n\nSolving $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7-x & =0 \\\\ x & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the extremum of the velocity function occurs when $x=7\\,m.$\n\nWe now test the extreme value using the first derivative test. We summarize the necessary information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(0,7)$ | $7$ | $(7,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $6$ |  | $8$ |\n| $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}$ | $1>0$ | $0$ | $-1<0$ |\n| $v$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ |\n\nWe conclude that the velocity is maximized when $x=7\\,m.$\n\nNext, using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our initial equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=7-x.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x=\\int (7-x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=7x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=\\sqrt{6}\\,m/s$ when $x=0\\,m,$ we can find the value of the constant $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =7x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(\\sqrt{6})^{2} & =7(0)-\\frac{1}{2}(0)^{2}+C \\\\ C & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =7x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+3 \\\\ {v}^{2} & =14x-{x}^{2}+6 \\\\ v & =\\pm \\sqrt{-{x}^{2}+14x+6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can disregard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}+14x+6}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the maximum velocity:\n\n$$\n{v}_{max}=v(7)=\\sqrt{-(7)^{2}+14(7)+6}=\\sqrt{55}\\,m/s\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-561bb6d9-fdfa-583e-add7-ec5538f0b8c0"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-561bb6d9-fdfa-583e-add7-ec5538f0b8c0" :answer-field/id #uuid "561bb6d9-fdfa-583e-add7-ec5538f0b8c0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-52f39f9c-16d0-5e71-b870-45274fd72ab1" :answer-field/choices ["answer-330011e7-cec4-558b-83ce-0451e10c41da" "answer-70c5036d-e847-5a0e-bf6c-48538005df98" "answer-7e188a6e-5859-50df-8694-4924096a1526" "answer-52f39f9c-16d0-5e71-b870-45274fd72ab1" "answer-dd5d7801-28ac-5ea0-88d2-5bd0cb858a80"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-330011e7-cec4-558b-83ce-0451e10c41da" :answer/id #uuid "330011e7-cec4-558b-83ce-0451e10c41da" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\sqrt{3}\\,m/s" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-70c5036d-e847-5a0e-bf6c-48538005df98" :answer/id #uuid "70c5036d-e847-5a0e-bf6c-48538005df98" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5\\,m/s" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7e188a6e-5859-50df-8694-4924096a1526" :answer/id #uuid "7e188a6e-5859-50df-8694-4924096a1526" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7\\,m/s" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-52f39f9c-16d0-5e71-b870-45274fd72ab1" :answer/id #uuid "52f39f9c-16d0-5e71-b870-45274fd72ab1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{55}\\,m/s" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dd5d7801-28ac-5ea0-88d2-5bd0cb858a80" :answer/id #uuid "dd5d7801-28ac-5ea0-88d2-5bd0cb858a80" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{61}\\,m/s" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186180703 :tutorial/content "We've seen how to calculate a particle's velocity and displacement when its acceleration is a function of *displacement.* In this lesson, we'll use this knowledge to compute various properties of a particle in motion.  \n\nSuppose a particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis. Its displacement from the origin is $x\\,\\rm{m},$ its velocity is $v\\,\\rm{m/s},$ and its acceleration is $2x\\,\\rm{m/s}^2.$ \n\n![](images/52/5222b12a7f08a33dc981ac95bd717cce945e1d2e3565fb3631f108fd0f307a12.png)\n\nFurthermore, suppose $P$ has velocity $2\\,\\rm{m/s}$ when its displacement is $\\sqrt{3}\\,\\rm{m}.$ \n\nLet's use this information to determine at which values of $x$ the particle is instantaneously at rest.\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $2x\\,\\rm{m/s}^2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}t} =2x.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}t}= \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}v^2\\right),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}v^2\\right) = 2x.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x} \\left(\\dfrac12v^2\\right) \\textrm{d}x\n=\n\\int 2x \\:\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\dfrac12v^2 = x^2+ C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=2\\,\\rm{m/s}$ when $x=\\sqrt 3\\,\\rm{m},$ we can find the value of the constant $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & ={x}^{2}+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(2)^{2} & =(\\sqrt{3})^{2}+C \\\\ 2 & =3+C \\\\ C & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac12v^2 = x^2-1.\n$$\n\nFinally, the particle is instantaneously at rest when $v(x) = 0\\,\\rm{m/s}.$ We find the displacement of the particle when $P$ is instantaneously at rest by substituting $v=0$ in the equation above and solving for $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & ={x}^{2}-1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(0)^{2} & ={x}^{2}-1 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =1 \\\\ x & =\\pm 1\\,m\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186179847 :activity/expected-seconds 1200.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186371071 :answer-field/choices 17592186371072] [:db/retract 17592186246762 :answer-field/choices 17592186246094] [:db/retract 17592186246762 :answer-field/choices 17592186246134] [:db/retract 17592186246762 :answer-field/choices 17592186246229] [:db/retract 17592186246762 :answer-field/choices 17592186244344] [:db/retract 17592186246762 :answer-field/choices 17592186245454] {:db/id 17592186119857 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-d77196fa-8d44-5cad-922e-bddbeed8f53e"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186119858 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-a78df4d7-9e4a-5ba6-b17f-e12077b05fb4"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-a78df4d7-9e4a-5ba6-b17f-e12077b05fb4" :question/id #uuid "a78df4d7-9e4a-5ba6-b17f-e12077b05fb4" :question/math-academy-id "q-76325" :question/problem "The vectors ${a}_{1},$ ${a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are given below. Determine whether $B=\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}.$ If that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ where ${a}_{3}={x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}.$\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We want to determine if $B$ is indeed a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}.$ So, we want to find ${x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in R$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{1}}+{x}_{2}{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{2}}={{[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{3}}.\n$$\n\nThis equation is equivalent to the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=5 \\\\ -{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce the augmented matrix of the system to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 8\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the RREF matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns. So,  $B$ is indeed a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}.$\nAlso, the matrix tells us that ${x}_{1}=1$ and ${x}_{2}=4,$ meaning that\n\n$$\n1\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{1}}+4\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{2}}={{[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{3}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=1\\cdot 4=4.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-46d3f8dc-aba1-58f4-a862-47da2d463904"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-46d3f8dc-aba1-58f4-a862-47da2d463904" :answer-field/id #uuid "46d3f8dc-aba1-58f4-a862-47da2d463904" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-422e2ec5-20ff-549c-9dc2-cfe71b65c008" :answer-field/choices ["answer-86784b09-cfa3-589f-b8d3-d3ab6f43882a" "answer-b9b14b1e-a851-5ba2-94dc-2cad1dd6e6f1" "answer-422e2ec5-20ff-549c-9dc2-cfe71b65c008" "answer-ef0bf52d-906e-5f34-9ed4-b19de3addea8" "answer-806a2215-b8ec-5aa9-aeab-b5dd3e906ac7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-86784b09-cfa3-589f-b8d3-d3ab6f43882a" :answer/id #uuid "86784b09-cfa3-589f-b8d3-d3ab6f43882a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b9b14b1e-a851-5ba2-94dc-2cad1dd6e6f1" :answer/id #uuid "b9b14b1e-a851-5ba2-94dc-2cad1dd6e6f1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-422e2ec5-20ff-549c-9dc2-cfe71b65c008" :answer/id #uuid "422e2ec5-20ff-549c-9dc2-cfe71b65c008" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ef0bf52d-906e-5f34-9ed4-b19de3addea8" :answer/id #uuid "ef0bf52d-906e-5f34-9ed4-b19de3addea8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-806a2215-b8ec-5aa9-aeab-b5dd3e906ac7" :answer/id #uuid "806a2215-b8ec-5aa9-aeab-b5dd3e906ac7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186371070 :question/problem "The vectors ${a}_{1},$ ${a}_{2},$ ${a}_{3},$ and ${a}_{4}$ are given below. Determine whether $B=\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3},{a}_{4}\\}.$ If that is the case, find ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3},$ where ${a}_{4}={x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}.$\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{4}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We want to determine if $B$ is indeed a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3},{a}_{4}\\}.$ So, we want to find ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}\\in R$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{1}}+{x}_{2}{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{2}}+{x}_{3}{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{3}}={{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{4}}.\n$$\n\nThis equation is equivalent to the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{3}=2 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}=-4 \\\\ -9{x}_{1}-8{x}_{3}=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce the augmented matrix of the system to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -2 & -4 \\\\ -9 & 0 & -8 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -9 & 0 & -8 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+9{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 17\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & -15 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 17\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the reduced matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st, $2$nd, and $3$rd columns. So,  $B$ is indeed a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3},{a}_{4}\\}.$\nAlso, the matrix tells us that ${x}_{1}=-15,$ ${x}_{2}=0,$ and ${x}_{3}=17,$ meaning that\n\n$$\n(-15)\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{1}}+0\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{2}}+17\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{3}}={{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{4}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=(-15)+0+17=2.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186371071 :answer-field/choices ["answer-684b58dc-c7bc-5e7a-ad38-d7e82bd7f00b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-684b58dc-c7bc-5e7a-ad38-d7e82bd7f00b" :answer/id #uuid "684b58dc-c7bc-5e7a-ad38-d7e82bd7f00b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3},{a}_{4}\\}$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-d77196fa-8d44-5cad-922e-bddbeed8f53e" :question/id #uuid "d77196fa-8d44-5cad-922e-bddbeed8f53e" :question/math-academy-id "q-110851" :question/problem "Consider the vectors ${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],$ ${a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],$ and ${a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}15 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ Find a basis $B$ of $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find a subset of linearly independent vectors. So, we start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}7 & 3 & 15 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, our matrix is already in a row echelon form.\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to linearly independent vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the $1$st and $2$nd columns are pivot columns, while the $3$rd column is a non-pivot column.\n\nThe $1$st and $2$nd columns from the original matrix are ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}.$ So, a basis of $S$ is $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\},$ that is to say, \n\n$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-de1a4479-26db-5da3-9251-44ec64871f06"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-de1a4479-26db-5da3-9251-44ec64871f06" :answer-field/id #uuid "de1a4479-26db-5da3-9251-44ec64871f06" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-d81d6455-dfab-5856-82d0-2c4f85caa5c0" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d19e6191-26d5-53a3-95c7-65dc2640adef" "answer-991e9362-9655-5cf0-a0be-aaa13d573c9f" "answer-28f8bb97-2bc4-5dc8-af62-80dc0012c72c" "answer-d81d6455-dfab-5856-82d0-2c4f85caa5c0" "answer-769b2e11-b333-500c-84a5-51aa4d8c16b8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d19e6191-26d5-53a3-95c7-65dc2640adef" :answer/id #uuid "d19e6191-26d5-53a3-95c7-65dc2640adef" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}15 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-991e9362-9655-5cf0-a0be-aaa13d573c9f" :answer/id #uuid "991e9362-9655-5cf0-a0be-aaa13d573c9f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-28f8bb97-2bc4-5dc8-af62-80dc0012c72c" :answer/id #uuid "28f8bb97-2bc4-5dc8-af62-80dc0012c72c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}15 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d81d6455-dfab-5856-82d0-2c4f85caa5c0" :answer/id #uuid "d81d6455-dfab-5856-82d0-2c4f85caa5c0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-769b2e11-b333-500c-84a5-51aa4d8c16b8" :answer/id #uuid "769b2e11-b333-500c-84a5-51aa4d8c16b8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186247171 :question/problem "Consider the vectors ${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],$ and ${a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ Find a basis $B$ of $\\text{span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find a subset of linearly independent vectors. So, we start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, our matrix is already in a row echelon form.\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to linearly independent vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the $1$st and $3$rd columns are pivot columns, while the $2$nd column is a non-pivot column.\n\nThe $1$st and $3$rd columns from the original matrix are ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{3}.$ So, a basis of $S$ is $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{3}\\},$ that is to say, \n\n$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186246762 :answer-field/correct "answer-8f4f12a7-86f7-5632-aa12-4ebf32e0df03" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3cc5baa7-58c6-5fb1-bb5b-bc5d2809c3fd" "answer-81e78e16-327e-5283-9ae6-8297686d3018" "answer-8f4f12a7-86f7-5632-aa12-4ebf32e0df03" "answer-316d5931-7999-53f3-ad90-06c5ea96ca28" "answer-8961892e-bd58-5202-80e6-b3295e996c34"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3cc5baa7-58c6-5fb1-bb5b-bc5d2809c3fd" :answer/id #uuid "3cc5baa7-58c6-5fb1-bb5b-bc5d2809c3fd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-81e78e16-327e-5283-9ae6-8297686d3018" :answer/id #uuid "81e78e16-327e-5283-9ae6-8297686d3018" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8f4f12a7-86f7-5632-aa12-4ebf32e0df03" :answer/id #uuid "8f4f12a7-86f7-5632-aa12-4ebf32e0df03" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-316d5931-7999-53f3-ad90-06c5ea96ca28" :answer/id #uuid "316d5931-7999-53f3-ad90-06